        ":question/id": "ccbaa794-afb5-5200-b91c-e8c3a1f38e0d",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-280352",
        ":question/problem": "Let $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\u27e81,5,0\u27e9$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}=\u27e8-2,4,0\u27e9.$ Find $\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}).$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})=\u27e8$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,0\u27e9$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a-3b)=c\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)=2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b) & =2\u27e81,5,0\u27e9-3\u27e8-2,4,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82,10,0\u27e9+\u27e86,-12,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e88,\\,-2,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
